  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain moves in late tonight from west to east. \nLocally heavy rainfall may occur late tonight and Monday \nmorning, but flooding is not expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... A soaking rainfall is in store for\nmany areas. Though there could be a few light showers or \nsprinkles late this afternoon and early this evening, the \nsoaking rain kicks in around dawn Monday. The rain could be \nfairly heavy at times especially from about 5am to noon. The \nrain becomes a bit more off and on and showery in nature later \nin the day and into Monday evening. There could be an embedded \nrumble of thunder Monday, but most of the instability is aloft \nand don't expect anything remotely strong. For rainfall totals \nfrom early Monday through Monday evening, feel pretty confident \nin at least one quarter inch for most areas. However, \npinpointing the heavier axis of rain where up to around an inch \ncould fall is a bit tougher, as models are still in poor \nagreement. In general, look for one quarter to three quarters of\nan inch of rain around the area with local amounts around an \ninch. This won't be enough to cause flooding. Rain begins \ntapering off for good late Monday night, though we could have \nsome fog developing.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Another round of rain Wednesday afternoon into \nThursday as a cold front crosses the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... As the low pressure system exits \nover the Maritimes, the next low pressure system centered over \nthe Hudson Bay begins to track E. The associated cold front is \nexpected to move into the region Wednesday evening and exit \nThursday afternoon. Ensemble models have been trending towards a\ndrier solution with isolated showers. Little QPF is expected \nwith these showers. There is a possibility for a few rumbles of \nthunder but nothing severe.",
